nsulated flash mixture which is rotating at 300 rpm..."\n        *   "With the help of graphite electrode DC is supplied to the sample kept in the plastic tank."\n        *   "As PPMS contain high total solid content, three part of water is added to PPMS in order to make slurry condition..."\n        *   "The process in which thermal energy is generated by passing electric current through organic materials is known as ohmic heating (Varghese et al., 2014). The motion of solid particles suspended in a liquid, under the influence of an electrical field is known as electro-osmosis (Mahmoud et al., 2010)."\n        *   The method is electrohydrolysis (using electricity/DC current to solubilize matter).\n    *   2.3 Anaerobic biodegradability tests: Reactor setup.\n    *   2.4 Analytical methods: Standard protocols.\n    *   2.5 Instrumental characterization: FESEM, FT-IR, XRD.\n\n3.  **Evaluate Mechanical Treatment:**\n    *   Does the methodology describe a mechanical treatment that alters the substrate size/structure directly?\n    *   The title says "Prerequisite – An electrohydrolysis pretreatment...".\n    *   Section 2.2 describes "schematic representation of electrohydrolysis pretreatment setup".\n    *   It mentions "flash mixture which is rotating at 300 rpm" inside the plastic tank.\n    *   It describes the action of the DC current and ions moving towards electrodes (electrophoresis, ohmic heating, electro-osmosis).\n    *   Does this count as "mechanically alters the substrate" (size reduction, structural disruption) or is it purely chemical/electrical?\n    *   The prompt definition of "mechanically alters the substrate" includes: "size reduction, structural disruption or similar".\n    *   The method is electrohydrolysis. It uses electricity to break bonds and disintegrate particles (as stated in the intro text: "break the bonds between polymers induced by application of current through electrodes"). However, the *physical process* described in the methodo